  "ID": "13",
  "Context/Background": "LINE1 (L1) comprises 17% of the human genome, with 50-150 full-length sequences capable of retrotransposition. Although largely inactive in normal somatic tissues, tumorigenesis leads to L1 derepression and overexpression of its RNA binding chaperone protein, ORF1p. A potential cancer biomarker, ORF1p expression is a hallmark of multiple cancers and an early event in precursor lesions.  Previous work in pancreatic cancer showed that repetitive elements, including L1, are able to trigger IFN-I activation through “viral mimicry” processes. In vitro and in vivo studies identified cytoplasmic dsRNA, RNA:DNA hybrids or dsDNA as potential molecules produced by retrotransposons expression and activity and able to trigger type-1 interferon (IFN-1) response. Moreover, the L1 ORF1p protein itself was shown to “mask” dsRNA L1 adducts decreasing the activation of IFN-1 mediated responses, inducing an opposite effect from the one triggered by L1 mRNA expression.\n\nWe conducted differential proteomic expression analysis accounting for tumor purity between ORF1p-high and ORF1p-low samples for each CPTAC cancer type (except GBM), identifying tumor specific and pan-cancer associations.  After filtering for proteins with an adjusted P < 0.2 in at least four of the cohorts, we identified a common signature of 17 proteins differentially expressed in ORF1p-high samples.  ",
  "Prompt": "These 15 proteins were up regulated in ORF1p high tumors: TFB2M, XPO5, MSH6, RFC4, PPM1G, CAD, LRPPRC, SLIRP, PDCD11, NKRF, SLC4A1AP, ESF1, DDX52, TBL3, BYSL. These 2 proteins were down regulated: SELENBP1, VPS13C.\nOut of the common signature of 17 proteins differentially expressed in ORF1p-high samples, 15 proteins were up in ORF1p high tumors: TFB2M, XPO5, MSH6, RFC4, PPM1G, CAD, LRPPRC, SLIRP, PDCD11, NKRF, SLC4A1AP, ESF1, DDX52, TBL3, BYSL; and 2 proteins were down: SELENBP1, VPS13C.  Please run ORA and search GO BP,CC,MF for these proteins (include all proteins in one ORA.)  Then, highlight important proteins and molecular pathways and functions that are most relevant given my study (ORF1p high, cancer, viral mimicry, IFN-1 activation or repression).  If multiple proteins are involved in similar processes you may group them and mention them together.  Also, please highlight any proteins that might be involved in repression of IFN signaliing, inflammation or viral defense processes. \n",
